---
// A summary of bxGenesis/start/rawBisosCBMs/README.org, which stays the source.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro"

const readmeUrl = "https://github.com/bxGenesis/start/blob/main/rawBisosCBMs/README.org"
const layering = `+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| 3. Niching + CapabilityBPOs  (later)                                 |
|    a real server's configuration and scale, over the generic CBS     |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| 2. Generic CBM pip packages  (bisos.kafka, bisos.grafana, ...)       |
|    the base layer: each capability installed, minimal, as x.here     |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| 1. Raw-BISOS platform  (raw-bisos.sh, from a Fresh-Debian)           |
|    /bisos, bystar, Blee, the venvs, bro_rawBisos as /bisos/platform  |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+`
const regression = `sysCbmManage.cs -i fullyMaterialize

# which walks, for collective/hereWeb:
ftoBranchProc.spcs -i fto_forwardToLeaves --cmndName=cbm_status
ftoBranchProc.spcs -i fto_forwardToLeaves --cmndName=cbm_materialize`
---
<Base title="rawBisosCBMs">
  <h1>rawBisosCBMs</h1>
  <p>How the capabilities of a Raw-BISOS platform are built. A summary of the
    <a href={readmeUrl}>rawBisosCBMs README</a>, which remains the source.</p>

  <h2>The Layering</h2>
  <pre>{layering}</pre>
  <p>
    <code>raw-bisos.sh</code> turns a Fresh-Debian into Raw-BISOS and ends at the platform; no capability is
    materialized by it. Each capability is then packaged once, generically, as a <code>bisos.&lt;name&gt;</code>
    pip package: a starting point to be configured, brought up in a minimal, out-of-the-box form that every raw
    platform gets the same way. What makes a deployment real (scale, site settings, security) is to come from
    CapabilityBPOs applied by niching.
  </p>

  <h2>What a CBM Is</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>CBA</strong> --- Capability Bundle Abstraction: the framework, <code>bisos.capability</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>CBS</strong> --- Capability Bundle Specification: one capability's sboms, systemd units and assembly (<code>&lt;name&gt;-cbs.pcs</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>CBM</strong> --- Capability Bundle Materialization: a CBS turned into running state on this host.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A CBM <em>leaf</em> lives at <code>/bisos/platform/sys/cbm/&lt;realm&gt;/&lt;capabilityType&gt;/&lt;name&gt;/</code>
    and points at a CBS (<code>cbmProc.spcs</code>), with a gate (<code>cbmProc.control</code>) and an outcome
    log (<code>cbmProc.status</code>). The gate is <em>enabled</em> (materialized with the tree),
    <em>available</em> (planted, not materialized automatically) or <em>disabled</em> (skipped). The
    <a href="/capabilities/">Capabilities</a> tables show exactly this.
  </p>

  <h2>The .here Convention</h2>
  <p>
    Every hereWeb capability is reachable as <code>http://&lt;name&gt;.here</code>: a fake domain in
    <code>/etc/hosts</code> on a <code>127.0.22.x</code> address that <code>bisos.banna</code> assigns once to the
    capability's port, served by nginx proxying to the program on <code>127.0.0.1</code>. Nothing listens beyond
    loopback, and every raw platform gets the same names, addresses and ports: an out-of-the-box experience,
    and regression-testable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Logins follow the rawBisos convention: user <code>admin</code>, password the service's name. Insecure by
    design; securing a host is a separate layer.
  </p>

  <h2>Regression</h2>
  <p>CBM regression is managed by <code>bisos.capability</code>:</p>
  <pre>{regression}</pre>
  <p>
    Each enabled leaf invokes its CBS with <code>cbs_materialize</code> and appends the outcome to its
    <code>cbmProc.status</code>, in the order of <code>branchesOrdered</code>. This site is the last leaf of that
    walk, so it is rebuilt after every other capability and its snapshot shows their outcome. Read the status
    lines rather than the exit status: a failed Cmnd still exits 0.
  </p>
</Base>
